course: dictionary
lesson: 7455
title: One word, two meanings: poorly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Poorly» has two main meanings, and the sentence around it tells you which one. Most of the time it means badly, not well: «The team played poorly.» Here the clue is «played», so the team did not play well.
explain: In British and Irish English, «poorly» can also mean ill or unwell. «Grandad is poorly today» means Grandad is not feeling well. Ask yourself: is this about how well something was done, or about how a person feels?
explain: Look at «poorly paid», which means getting too little money, and «poorly lit», which means not enough light. «Albino animals often see poorly» means they do not see well. Swap in «badly» and see if it still makes sense; if it does, «poorly» means badly.
fact: «Poorly» means badly, not well, as in «The team played poorly».
fact: In British and Irish English, «poorly» can mean ill or unwell, as in «feel poorly».
fact: «Poorly paid» means getting too little money.
fact: «Did poorly on the test» means did not score well on the test.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping in «badly»; if the sentence still makes sense, «poorly» means badly.
exercise: «The team played poorly.» What does poorly mean here? | Happily | Very fast | Loudly | *Badly, not well
exercise: «Grandad is poorly today.» In British English, what does poorly mean? | Very happy | Full of energy | Very tall | *Ill or unwell
exercise: «Poorly paid» means | *Getting too little money | Getting a lot of money | Paid by post | Paid in the morning
test: Tom says he feels poorly, so he stays in bed. What does poorly mean here? | Playing a game badly | *Unwell, feeling ill | Very cold | Badly painted
test: «She did poorly on the test.» What does poorly suggest? | She finished the test very early | She did the test in the rain | *She did badly and did not score well | She got top marks
exam: Which sentence uses poorly to mean unwell? | «The bike was poorly made.» | «The room was poorly lit.» | *«My mum's poorly, so she is resting.» | «The team played poorly in the second half.»
